in only after your fix here is verified and admitted.\\\n  \\ The six\\nfailures below will not reproduce in your own environment right now.\\\n  \\ Your\\nfix must be correct by construction and verified by your existing suite\\n\\\n  passing cleanly today \\u2014 do not hardcode today's specific behavior anywhere,\\n\\\n  and do not weaken any assertion's actual guarantee, only what it assumes\\nabout\\\n  \\ which nodes are currently wired.\\n\\n- `test_the_compiled_graph_registers_every_declared_unit_branch`\\\n  \\ computes\\n  `expected = branch_destinations(source, available)` from `available\\\n  \\ =\\n  tuple(sorted(binding_inventory()))` (deliberately narrow, per P-N31-001's\\n\\\n  \\  own instruction not to change what `binding_inventory()` returns), then\\n  asserts\\\n  \\ it equals `registered`, read from the real compiled graph's edges.\\n  Once D08/D09/D12/D14/D91's\\\n  \\ guards really do route to D17 (and M05's to\\n  D16) in the real compiled graph,\\\n  \\ `registered` correctly contains those\\n  destinations while `expected` \\u2014\\\n  \\ computed from the frozen narrow\\n  `binding_inventory()` \\u2014 does not, so the\\\n  \\ two can no longer be equal by\\n  construction. Fix this the same way P-N20-001\\\n  \\ fixes the analogous topology\\n  test: derive `available` for this comparison from\\\n  \\ whatever the real\\n  compiled graph's own node set is (`set(compiled.get_graph().nodes)`)\\n\\\n  \\  intersected with what `branch_destinations`/`deferred_destinations`\\n  actually\\\n  \\ need, rather than from `binding_inventory()`'s always-narrow\\n  return value \\u2014\\\n  \\ the test's real job is proving `register_unit_path` wired\\n  exactly the destinations\\\n  \\ that truly have bodies in whatever the production\\n  compile point actually used,\\\n  \\ which is a self-consistency check against the\\n  compiled graph itself, not a\\\n  \\ hardcoded external list frozen at whichever\\n  generation last touched this file.\\n\\\n